fine. Let me check `_parse_json` signature: `_parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True)`. With valid JSON, returns parsed object. Good.\n\nNote: ExtractorError stores `expected` attribute. `exc_info.value.expected` should be True.\n\nAlso note: ExtractorError's `__str__` includes the msg. Let me check: `ExtractorError.__str__` returns something like `'[%s] %s' % (self.video_id or self.ie_key()..., self.msg)`? Hmm:\n\n```python\nclass ExtractorError(YoutubeDLError):\n    def __init__(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False, cause=None, video_id=None):\n        ...\n        self.msg = msg\n    def __str__(self):\n        if not self.video_id:\n            ...\n        return ...\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        \"\"\"Add video ID to error message\"\"\"\n        if self.video_id is None:\n            return self.msg\n        return '[%s] %s' % (self.video_id, self.msg)\n```\n\nHmm, not exactly; there's tracebacks etc. Anyway, checking `exc_info.value.msg == 'Geo restricted content'` is safer. ExtractorError has `.msg` attribute. Let me verify via get_info(\"ExtractorError\").\n\n**Test 3: non-403 HTTPError re-raised**\n\n```python\ndef test_other_http_error_reraised(monkeypatch):\n    video_json = _video_json()\n    http_error = compat_HTTPError(url, 500, 'Internal Server Error', {}, io.BytesIO(b'{}'))\n    streams_exc = ExtractorError('boom', cause=http_error)\n    ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, video_json, streams_exc=streams_exc)\n    with pytest.raises(ExtractorError) as exc_info:\n        ie._real_extract(VIDEO_URL)\n    assert exc_info.value is streams_exc  # re-raised as-is\n```\n\nHmm, `raise` re-raises the same exception object. `exc_info.value is streams_exc` - should be true. Actually ExtractorError might be wrapped? No, `raise` re-raises. But note: ExtractorError's constructor with cause... fine.\n\nWait, one subtlety: `compat_HTTPError` - in youtube_dl.compat, `compat_HTTPError = urllib.error.HTTPError` (or urllib2.HTTPError in py2). Constructing HTTPError requires hdrs to be something with... it accepts any object; hdrs is stored. Fine.\n\n**Test 4: no formats + geo blocked -> geo restricted**\n\n```python\ndef test_geo_restricted(monkeypatch):\n    video_json = _video_json(is_geo_blocked=True)\n    ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, video_json, streams_json={'streams': {}})\n    with pytest.raises(ExtractorError) as exc_info:\n        ie._real_extract(VIDEO_URL)\n    assert exc_info.value.expected\n    assert 'copyright' in str(exc_info.value)  # or check msg\n```\n\nHmm, `raise_geo_restricted` in this youtube-dl version - need to check what it does. In youtube-dl (2019+):\n\n```python\ndef raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None, metadata_available=False):\n    \"\"\"Raise a GeoRestrictedError\"\"\"\n    raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, ...)\n```\n\nHmm wait, GeoRestrictedError is a subclass of ExtractorError with expected=True. Let me check. In youtube-dl:\n\n```python\nclass GeoRestrictedError(ExtractorError):\n    \"\"\"Geographic restriction Error exception.\n\n    This exception may be thrown when a video is not available from your\n    geographic location due to geographic restrictions imposed by a website.\n    It is expected to be thrown during extraction.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, msg, countries=None, cause=None, **kwargs):\n        countries = countries or []\n        new_msg = (\n            '%s. This video is available in %s only' % (msg, ', '.join(countries))\n            if countries else msg)\n        kwargs['expected'] = True\n        super(GeoRestrictedError, self).__init__(new_msg, cause=cause, **kwargs)\n```\n\nAnd `raise_geo_restricted`:\n\n```python\ndef raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None, metadata_available=False):\n    \"\"\"Raise a GeoRestrictedError\"\"\"\n    raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, countries=countries)\n```\n\nHmm, but newer versions check `self.get_param('geo_bypass')`... Actually in yt-dlp there's more logic